# Finish the LM Studio tooling work in ~/dp/git/rvw

Handoff written 2026-08-21 on the M4 Air, partway through the task. Everything
described under "Already done" is on disk and passing; everything under "What is
left" is not started. Work on whichever machine you are on, but read "Machine
differences" first, because this was verified on the M4 and the M3 is not in the
same state.

## The project

`~/dp/git/rvw` is a local macOS listening assistant: it captures microphone and
system audio, transcribes with MLX Whisper, and answers questions about the last
minute of conversation with a local LLM served by LM Studio. Read `AGENTS.md`
first, then `doc/phase2` for the most recent design decisions and measurements.
`prompts/base` is the specification and its CURRENT STATE section is kept up to
date; Phases 0 to 2 are done.

Run the tests with `util/run_tests.sh` (unittest, no pytest in the venv). There
are 113 and they all pass right now. `util/run_tests.sh -k list_models` runs one
file.

## How this task started

I was asked two questions: how to see what models are on the machine, and
whether LM Studio should be installed as part of `util/init.sh`. The answer to
the second was no, it already is: `init.sh` line 7 runs `init_local_models.sh`,
whose `main()` calls `install_llmster_if_missing`. The user then approved two
pieces of work, one finished and one not:

1. `util/list_models.sh`, a single command showing both model stores. DONE.
2. A LaunchAgent so the LM Studio server survives a reboot. NOT STARTED.

Number 2 exists because `lms server` offers only `start`, `stop` and `status`,
with no boot option, and `init_local_models.sh` starts the server once at setup
time. After a reboot the assistant starts normally and every question fails
until someone runs `lms server start` by hand.

## Already done, uncommitted

    ?? util/assistant_settings.sh      new
    ?? util/list_models.sh             new
    ?? test/shell_script_testing.py    new
    ?? test/test_list_models.py        new
     M util/init_local_models.sh       uses assistant_settings.sh now
     M test/test_permission_probe.py   uses shell_script_testing.py now

`util/assistant_settings.sh` is a sourced library, not a program. It holds
`read_assistant_setting <name>`, which reads any module level variable out of
`src/rvw/config.py`, and `read_llm_server_port`, which parses the port out of
`config.llm_base_url`. It exists so that the identifiers and the port are
written down once, in `config.py`, and every shell script asks rather than
repeating them. `init_local_models.sh` was moved onto it and its hardcoded
`llm_server_port=1234` is gone. Follow this: do not hardcode a port, an
identifier or a model name anywhere in the work below.

`util/list_models.sh` reports the LM Studio store (`lms ls`, `lms ps`), then the
identifiers the assistant asks for, then the Hugging Face cache. Its real output
on the M4:

    LM Studio, downloaded
    qwen3.6-35b-a3b    35B    qwen3_5_moe    20.43 GB    Local   LOADED
    text-embedding-nomic-embed-text-v1.5    Nomic BERT    84.11 MB

    LM Studio, loaded right now
    meeting-assistant    qwen3.6-35b-a3b    IDLE    20.43 GB    34560    1h

    Identifiers the assistant asks for
    OK   meeting-assistant is served, so EXPLAIN and CLARIFY will reach it
    FAIL meeting-vision is not served, so INTERPRET_SCREEN would be answered by
         whatever model is loaded

    Hugging Face cache, which LM Studio never sees
         mlx-community/whisper-large-v3-turbo          1.6G
         pyannote/speaker-diarization-community-1     32.8M

`test/shell_script_testing.py` is the harness that lets a python test drive the
functions inside a bash script. Reuse it; do not invent a second way.

## What is left

### 1. util/start_llm_server.sh

One script that makes sure the llmster daemon and the OpenAI compatible server
are both up, and does nothing if they already are. It is what the LaunchAgent
runs, and it must also replace the two functions that do this today inside
`init_local_models.sh`, `start_llmster_daemon` and `start_llm_server`, so that
the logic exists in one place. `init_local_models.sh` supports `-dry` through
its `run_cmd`, so the extracted script needs its own `-dry` and
`init_local_models.sh` has to keep honouring it.

Read the port with `read_llm_server_port` from `assistant_settings.sh`.

Critical: invoke `lms` by absolute path, `$HOME/.lmstudio/bin/lms`. A LaunchAgent
runs with almost no environment and none of your shell profile, so a bare `lms`
will not be found at login. This is the same lesson already recorded in
`src/rvw/config.py`, where `lms_command` is absolute because `rvw.app` inherits
no shell PATH.

### 2. util/init_llm_autostart.sh

Generates and installs the LaunchAgent. Decisions already taken:

- label `ai.rvw.llm_server`, matching the `ai.rvw.assistant` bundle identifier
- plist written to `~/Library/LaunchAgents/ai.rvw.llm_server.plist`
- generated, not shipped as a static file, because it has to contain the
  absolute path of this checkout and the checkout is explicitly allowed to live
  anywhere; see the comment at the top of `helper/rvw_launcher.swift`
- `ProgramArguments` runs `util/start_llm_server.sh`
- `RunAtLoad` true, and no `KeepAlive`: the script starts the server and exits,
  the server itself is the llmster daemon's business
- output to `var/log/llm_server.launchagent.log`
- install with `launchctl bootout gui/$UID/ai.rvw.llm_server` (ignoring failure
  when it is not loaded) then `launchctl bootstrap gui/$UID <plist>`. Do not use
  `launchctl load`; it is deprecated and this is macOS 26.
- rerunning must be safe, and `-dry` must change nothing

Do not put this plist in `helper/`. The three plists there are Info.plists
embedded into binaries, an entirely different thing, and confusing them would be
easy.

### 3. util/init.sh

Add the autostart step. `init.sh` puts `util/` on PATH, so the existing lines
call bare script names; match that.

### 4. Verify it for real

    util/init_llm_autostart.sh
    launchctl print gui/$UID/ai.rvw.llm_server | head -20
    lms server stop
    launchctl kickstart -k gui/$UID/ai.rvw.llm_server
    sleep 5 && lms server status          # expect: running
    util/list_models.sh                   # expect: meeting-assistant served again

A real reboot is the only complete test. Ask the user before rebooting their
machine; do not do it unasked.

### 5. Document it

`AGENTS.md`, under Commands, gets `util/list_models.sh` and
`util/init_llm_autostart.sh`. Under Notes, record that the LM Studio server does
not survive a reboot on its own and that the LaunchAgent is what fixes it. Keep
the existing tone: short, factual, and say why rather than what.

## Facts you need that are not obvious

The endpoint lies about models. Asked for an identifier it does not serve, this
LM Studio build neither refuses nor loads anything: it answers with whatever
model is loaded and names that substitute in the response. Verified directly:
a request naming `totally-nonexistent-model-xyz` came back as
`"model": "meeting-assistant"`. `src/rvw/llm.py` guards both ends because of it
and `INTERPRET_SCREEN` checks before promising an answer. Never add a code path
that trusts this endpoint to serve what it was asked for.

Every script in `util/` ends with a bare `exit` on a line of its own, followed by
example invocations. That is the user's convention, it is deliberate, and it must
be left in place. It also means sourcing such a script would end the shell that
sourced it, so anything testable also carries

    if [[ ${BASH_SOURCE[0]} == "$0" ]]; then
            main "$@"
    fi

and the tests source a copy truncated at that `exit`. `sourceable_copy_of` in
`test/shell_script_testing.py` does all of it, including building a stand in
repository so the copied script still resolves `src/` and `.venv/` above itself.
`write_stand_in_command` puts a fake `lms` or `launchctl` on PATH; note that a
script which looks under `$HOME` for a command needs `run_bash_using(..., home=)`
as well, since PATH alone will not shadow it.

Never rebuild `bin/rvw.app` unnecessarily. Its ad hoc signature is pinned to the
launcher's cdhash and re-signing voids every macOS permission granted to it.
`helper/build_app.sh` already rebuilds only when its own sources change; leave
that alone.

## How to work

The user requires real TDD: write the failing test first, watch it fail for the
right reason, make it pass, then refactor. When I skipped straight to asserting,
two of my tests passed vacuously and hid a live bug for an hour. Prove RED before
you believe GREEN.

Other house rules that bite in this repo: snake_case; spaces, never tabs; no
unicode characters anywhere; log lines start with `OK `, `FAIL ` or `INFO `, and
`FAIL` is reserved for something actually wrong, never for a state working as
designed; functions under about 25 lines, with names that say what they do; no
comment explaining the next six lines when a named function would say it better;
no duplicated logic; paths relative to the script's own location, never absolute
roots and never a fallback search for a file in a second location.

Fix bugs under 50 lines immediately rather than asking. If a command's output
contains `FAIL`, investigate it rather than moving on.

## Machine differences

This was all verified on the M4 Air, 32 GB, hostname m4. There, all four macOS
permissions are granted, `meeting-assistant` is loaded, and no vision model
exists anywhere. On the M3, 96 GB, the permissions were last measured on
2026-08-16 as microphone and system audio granted, screen recording declined and
Hammerspoon without Accessibility; run `util/init_permissions.sh` and believe
what it says now rather than that note. `EXPLAIN` takes 78 s on the Air for a
60 s window, which is a model sizing problem recorded in `doc/phase2`, not
something for this task.

## When you are done

Run the full suite. Then, per the user's global rules, save the session
transcript to
`$dp/git/doc/prompts/YYYY-MM-DD_HH.mm.$HOSTNAME.$IDE_NAME.<task>`, redacting any
secret that appeared in a tool call, and offer a one line commit message in the
user's style: lowercase, descriptive prose, often ending with the host name, for
example "start the lm studio server at login with a launchagent on m3".
